Portico (portico-ha) is released under the MIT License (see LICENSE).

This repository does not vendor third-party source code. It uses the following third-party material, which is
distributed under its own terms and is not covered by the MIT License above.

Python integration (installed by Home Assistant, not bundled here)
  - qrcode (BSD-3-Clause) - listed in custom_components/portico/manifest.json
  - Home Assistant core (Apache-2.0) - the integration runs inside it

Transport binary (transport-rs)
  Built from the crates pinned in transport-rs/Cargo.lock and linked statically into the release binary.
  Each crate keeps its own license (predominantly MIT and/or Apache-2.0; ISC and BSD-style licenses for some,
  e.g. rustls and aws-lc-rs which is Apache-2.0 / ISC / OpenSSL-style). The direct dependencies include
  tokio, serde, serde_json, str0m, rustls, tokio-rustls, aws-lc-rs, if-addrs, md-5 and getrandom.
  See `cargo metadata` (or `cargo license`) for the full per-crate list.

Development tooling (downloaded at test time, not shipped)
  - ZXing core 3.5.3 (Apache-2.0) - scripts/zxing/Dec.java is a small program that calls it to decode QR codes in
    tests; the library itself is fetched by scripts/qr_decode_check.sh.
  - jsdom (MIT) - installed by scripts/build_test_venv.sh --node for the card and panel tests.
  - pytest and pytest-homeassistant-custom-component (MIT / Apache-2.0) - the Python test harness.

Names and data
  - Icon names in contract/icons_v1.json are Material Design Icons identifiers (Apache-2.0 / the MDI project);
    only the names are listed, no glyphs are bundled.
  - Test fixtures under transport-rs/tests/interop/fixtures contain SDP offers recorded from Chrome and Firefox;
    they are protocol traces, not code.
